# pipewire -- and the first thing to know about it is that SOUND IS THE HALF YOU
# CAN SEE, not the half that blocks the desktop.
#
# mutter takes pipewire for screencast and remote desktop. Without it the
# compositor builds, starts and draws, and then every screen-sharing path is
# simply absent: no portal capture, no remote desktop, and gnome-shell's own
# recorder does nothing. So this is not "the Sound panel's package with a
# screencast bonus" -- it is a compositor dependency that also carries audio.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env: versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh, so a desktop
# pin placed there is destroyed by the next `make pin`.
SRC_URL=https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/pipewire/pipewire/-/archive/1.4.7/pipewire-1.4.7.tar.bz2
SRC_SHA256=17599eab9cf1ac98bce41954057b1b66d16c99aef341e16d41dbc7302f4fa960
SRC_DIR=pipewire-1.4.7

# READ THE OPTION DEFAULTS BEFORE READING THIS LIST. pipewire's options are
# meson `feature` types and most of them default to 'auto', which means "build
# it if the dependency happens to be installed". That is the opposite of
# weston's trap, where everything defaults to true, and it is worse in one
# respect: an auto option produces a DIFFERENT ARTEFACT in different images
# without ever saying so. Every option below is therefore stated, including the
# ones whose value equals the default, so the package is a function of the
# recipe rather than of the builder image.
#
# -Dsession-managers=[] IS LOAD-BEARING AND MUST NOT BE REMOVED.
# It defaults to ['wireplumber'], which is a meson SUBPROJECT reference, not a
# system dependency -- meson would try to fetch wireplumber and build it inside
# this build. _scripts/build-meson.sh passes --wrap-mode=nodownload precisely so
# that cannot happen quietly, which turns this from a silent vendored copy into
# a hard configure failure. wireplumber is packaged separately and depends on
# this package; building it here would be a cycle as well as a duplicate.
#
# Measured rather than reasoned about -- removing the option gives, verbatim:
#     ERROR: Subproject wireplumber is buildable: NO
#     src/daemon/meson.build:37:12: ERROR: Automatic wrap-based subproject
#     downloading is disabled
# That pair of lines is what a meson `fallback:` looks like in this tree, and it
# is worth recognising on sight: every recipe here inherits --wrap-mode=nodownload,
# so an upstream fallback that other distributions never notice is, for Duct,
# either a hard stop or a package someone has to add.
#
# ENABLED, and why each one matters rather than what it is:
#   alsa, pipewire-alsa   the ALSA device backend, plus the libasound plugin
#                         that routes ALSA-only programs through pipewire.
#                         -Dpipewire-alsa=enabled also makes the alsa dependency
#                         REQUIRED rather than auto, so a missing alsa-lib fails
#                         configure instead of quietly producing a server with
#                         no sound devices.
#   udev                  device discovery. Left at auto this is exactly the
#                         ambient-detection problem described above.
#   dbus                  device reservation, and the portal handshake that
#                         screen sharing goes through
#   logind + logind-provider=libelogind
#                         pipewire asks logind whether the session is active
#                         before touching a device on a seat. The provider is
#                         named EXPLICITLY: the option defaults to 'libsystemd',
#                         and Duct's elogind installs a libsystemd.pc symlink,
#                         so the default would have silently resolved to elogind
#                         through a compatibility alias. Same outcome, arrived
#                         at by accident.
#   flatpak, gsettings, gsettings-pulse-schema
#                         sandbox client recognition and the
#                         pulseaudio-compatibility schema GNOME's volume
#                         controls read
#   v4l2, pipewire-v4l2   cameras. The SPA plugin needs kernel headers and
#                         libudev, not libv4l, so this costs no new package.
#
# DISABLED, with what each one would drag in:
#   bluez5                TRACKED FOLLOW-UP, NOT A SETTLED DECISION -- see the
#                         block at the end of this file. Bluetooth audio is
#                         approved and blocked only on bluez, which another
#                         chain owns.
#   gstreamer, gstreamer-device-provider   gstreamer
#   libpulse              PulseAudio, for tunnelling to ANOTHER pulse server.
#                         Nothing to do with pipewire-pulse, which is this
#                         package's own pulse server and is still built.
#   sndfile, opus, avahi, libcanberra, roc, lv2, libmysofa,
#   echo-cancel-webrtc, libffado, snap, ebur128, libcamera, sdl2
#                         one library each that no recipe in this tree provides
#   libusb                Bluetooth adapter quirks only (meson.build:379,
#                         "libusb (Bluetooth quirks)"), and reachable ONLY from
#                         the native HSP/HFP backend, which -Dbluez5=disabled
#                         switches off entirely. Stated separately from the list
#                         above because libusb IS packaged in this tree -- so
#                         this is a decision rather than an absence, and it is
#                         revisited with bluez5 below, not before.
#   readline              pw-cli line editing; readline is not packaged
#   raop                  AirPlay. openssl IS packaged, so auto would have
#                         enabled this -- disabled because nothing wants it,
#                         and stated because auto would not have been a choice.
#   x11, x11-xfixes       module-x11-bell. libX11 and libXfixes ARE packaged, so
#                         auto would have built it; Duct is Wayland-first and
#                         the X bell has no consumer here.
#   jack                  the JACK *client* SPA plugin, which needs libjack.
#                         pipewire-jack -- pipewire's own reimplementation of
#                         the JACK API, which needs nothing external -- stays at
#                         its default and is still built.
#   systemd, systemd-system-service, systemd-user-service
#                         Duct has elogind and no systemd. Named rather than
#                         left at auto for the same reason as logind-provider:
#                         elogind's libsystemd.pc symlink would otherwise make
#                         dependency('libsystemd') succeed.
#   selinux, tests, examples, man, docs
#                         nothing here needs them, and tests and examples are
#                         build time spent on binaries that are not installed
MESON_ARGS="-Dsession-managers=[] \
-Dalsa=enabled -Dpipewire-alsa=enabled -Dudev=enabled -Ddbus=enabled \
-Dlogind=enabled -Dlogind-provider=libelogind \
-Dflatpak=enabled -Dgsettings=enabled -Dgsettings-pulse-schema=enabled \
-Dv4l2=enabled -Dpipewire-v4l2=enabled \
-Dbluez5=disabled -Dgstreamer=disabled -Dgstreamer-device-provider=disabled \
-Dlibpulse=disabled -Dsndfile=disabled -Dopus=disabled -Davahi=disabled \
-Dlibusb=disabled -Dlibcanberra=disabled -Dreadline=disabled -Droc=disabled \
-Dlv2=disabled -Dlibmysofa=disabled -Decho-cancel-webrtc=disabled \
-Dlibffado=disabled -Dsnap=disabled -Debur128=disabled -Dlibcamera=disabled \
-Dsdl2=disabled -Dx11=disabled -Dx11-xfixes=disabled -Draop=disabled \
-Djack=disabled \
-Dsystemd=disabled -Dsystemd-system-service=disabled -Dsystemd-user-service=disabled \
-Dselinux=disabled -Dtests=disabled -Dexamples=disabled -Dman=disabled -Ddocs=disabled"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FOLLOW-UP: BLUETOOTH AUDIO. Written as a tracked item rather than as prose
# about what is or is not packaged, because that prose goes false the moment
# another chain merges -- which is exactly what happened to the sentence that
# used to be here, claiming bluez, sbc and libusb were all unpackaged.
#
# STATUS as of 2026-08-11: sbc is packaged (pkgs/sbc, this chain). bluez is
# owned by another chain and not yet merged. libusb is packaged. The blocker is
# bluez alone.
#
# WHAT THE RE-ENABLE IS. Not "delete -Dbluez5=disabled" -- the option's default
# is 'auto', and auto is the failure mode here rather than a convenience:
#
#   spa/meson.build:53-66 takes bluez, gio-2.0, gio-unix-2.0, glib-2.0 and sbc
#   with `required: get_option('bluez5')`, then loops over them:
#
#       if get_option('bluez5').enabled() and not dep.found()
#         error('bluez5 enabled, but dependency not found: ' + dep.name())
#       endif
#       bluez_deps_found = bluez_deps_found and dep.found()
#
#   Under 'enabled' a missing dependency is a NAMED configure error. Under
#   'auto' the same miss sets bluez_deps_found = false and the entire bluez5
#   plugin is dropped in silence -- a green build, a desktop that pairs a
#   headset, and no audio path to it. So the flag must become
#   -Dbluez5=enabled, and install.sh must assert libspa-bluez5.so the way it
#   already asserts libspa-alsa.so, for the same reason: SPA plugins are loaded
#   by filename at run time and nothing at build or install time misses one.
#
# REQUIRED versus MERELY BETTER, read from the build files rather than from a
# dependency page:
#
#   REQUIRED for -Dbluez5=enabled: bluez (>= 4.101), sbc, and glib/gio/dbus,
#   which are already dependencies of this package. That is the whole hard set.
#   sbc is not one codec among several -- spa/plugins/bluez5/meson.build builds
#   spa-codec-bluez5-sbc and spa-codec-bluez5-faststream against sbc_dep with NO
#   found() guard, and SBC is the codec every A2DP sink must implement. sbc
#   alone gives working Bluetooth audio.
#
#   OPTIONAL, each behind `if <dep>.found()` and each costing a new package:
#     aptX      libfreeaptx        better quality on Qualcomm sinks
#     LDAC      ldacBT-enc + -abr  better quality on Sony sinks
#     AAC       fdk-aac            better quality on Apple sinks
#     LC3plus   lc3plus            niche
#     Opus      opus               also gated on -Dopus, disabled above
#   Every one of these is an improvement on SBC for hardware that supports it,
#   and none is a prerequisite for sound. The recommendation is to ship SBC
#   alone and treat the rest as a separate decision with a named beneficiary.
#
#   TWO WORTH DECIDING EXPLICITLY RATHER THAN BY OMISSION:
#     LC3   liblc3. NOT a quality upgrade -- it is the mandatory codec for
#           Bluetooth LE Audio. Without it, classic A2DP works and LE Audio
#           devices do not. Newer headsets are increasingly LE-only, so this is
#           a hardware-support question, not an audiophile one.
#     G722  needs NOTHING. spa/plugins/bluez5/meson.build builds
#           spa-codec-bluez5-g722 with `dependencies : [ spa_dep ]` and the
#           option defaults to auto, so it arrives free with the re-enable. It
#           is the ASHA codec, which is how hearing aids connect.
#
#   ModemManager (-Dbluez5-backend-native-mm) defaults DISABLED upstream and is
#   the only bluez5 option another chain's package would satisfy. It routes HFP
#   calls through a modem; a desktop without one does not want it.
#
# COST HERE: a subversion bump, because the dependency list reaches
# TAPEBUILD.toml. Do not re-enable before bluez merges AND is published --
# merged is not installable, see the seeding note in the PR that added this.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
